-*   **Strengths:** Improves optimization robustness, handles large spatio-temporal divergence, recovers complex motion and fine details, reduces Gaussian count while improving metrics [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Slower training time; requires careful tuning of $m$ and thresholds; risk of divergence if applied to non-dead Gaussians [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Static and dynamic scene reconstruction with poor initialization or large motion; when baseline 3DGS exhibits floaters, blurring, or missing structures [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When training speed is critical and baseline performance is sufficient; when scene has no significant divergence or initialization issues [S1].
+*   **Performance:**
+    *   **Static Scenes (Mip-NeRF360, Random Init):** PSNR 25.16 (vs 24.74), SSIM 0.8511 (vs 0.8351), LPIPS 0.1974 (vs 0.2106), #GS 0.799M (vs 1.126M) [S1].
+    *   **Dynamic Scenes (Neural 3D Video):** PSNR 31.70 (vs 30.22), SSIM 0.9493 (vs 0.9443), LPIPS 0.1339 (vs 0.1360), #GS 1.733M (vs 2.806M) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves optimization robustness, handles large spatio-temporal divergence, reduces Gaussian count, and works as a plug-and-play module for 3DGS, 2DGS, 4DGS, CEM-4DGS, and 4DScaffold [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Slower training time; requires careful tuning of $m$ and thresholds to avoid divergence; relies on the assumption that dead Gaussians can converge via long-range gradients.
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** Proposed by T. Morales, N.-Q. Le-Pham, R. Atkins & B.-S. Hua in "TruncGradGS: Improved 3D Gaussian Splatting via Truncated Gradient Updates" [S1].
-*   **History:** Published in Eurographics 2026 [S1]. It builds on the differentiable rendering framework of 3D Gaussian Splatting [G].
-*   **Usage:** Used for static and dynamic scene reconstruction, particularly in challenging conditions with large spatio-temporal divergence [S1].
